Ladybird now shows each MP4 frame until the next frame in presentation
order starts, whatever its own sample duration says. Firefox does the
same: Its Moof::Moof() (MoofParser.cpp) sorts each fragment’s samples
by composition time and ends each one at the next one’s start, and
mp4parse’s create_sample_table() does the same for a sample table. And
Chrome’s VideoRendererAlgorithm::UpdateFrameStatistics() ends each
queued frame at the next frame’s timestamp, so the frame’s duration
never decides when it’s replaced. WebKit plays MP4 through AVFoundation,
so its source shows nothing comparable.

Firefox sorts a whole sample table at once. Ladybird looks only at the
16 samples on either side of each one in decode order instead, since a
few table entries can describe billions of samples.

The issue’s file, and a plain MP4 remuxed from it with “ffmpeg -c
copy” (which keeps the uneven decode times), each play 4 seconds with
no frames dropped in Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5. Ladybird
dropped 43 and 45 of 96 before this change, and drops none after it.
